A1. What does your wife do to your household? ~ MANAGE
A6. An artist who draws for JUDGE. ~ CROSBY
A11. What the girl said when her boy friend tried to kiss her in a canoe—Also an Island. ~ HELGOLAND
A12. Distinguished Englishmen like to become one of these (abr.). ~ MP
A14. One of the vowels repeated three times. ~ OOO
A15. What the boys would yell if you drove by in a 1910 model. ~ ICE
A16. What you are apt to see if you drink too much liquor (abr.). ~ MD
A17. Disfigure. ~ MAR
A19. Some one in wait for the errant husband. ~ WIFEY
A21. Something of use to movie comedians. ~ PIE
A22. What Willie did to the jam. ~ ATE
A23. Something Billy Sunday is all excited about. ~ SIN
A24. A wing of a house. ~ ELL
A25. Corruption. ~ TAINT
A27. Did you ever do this for the boys in the back room in the Good Old Days? ~ TREAT
A29. Some one having something to do with JUDGE—and who thinks it's a darn good magazine (abr.). ~ ED
A30. What JUDGE thinks about the Ku Klux Klan. ~ NG
A31. Harsh or severe. ~ RIGOR
A33. Where was the lamb chop Fido stole from the table? Also, to bring upon oneself. ~ INCUR
A34. Something which is often at sea (abr.). ~ USN
A35. A long, long time. ~ EON
A37. Something that goes round-and-round-and-round. ~ TOP
A39. Very marshy. ~ BOG
A40. Something old-fashioned people used to do in the home. ~ ABIDE
A42. A direction (abr.). ~ SSE
A43. A preposition—(You're on to this.) ~ ON
A44. Something miners go in for. ~ ORE
A45. The first girl who said,"Really, I've simply nothing to wear." ~ EVE
A47. Where you're apt to find yourself if you get too fresh with the "little woman"—(abr.). ~ ST
A48. They run at night in packs— like wolves. ~ KUKLUXERS
A51. Tapestry or something similar. ~ TAPIS
A52. A day in your life before you started doing Cross Word puzzles. ~ NATAL

D1. A familiar name for some one all song writers seem to love. ~ MAMMA
D2. A New England State (abr.). ~ NH
D3. Three of the vowels. ~ AEO
D4. What the light in the parlor does if her Dad is home. ~ GLOW
D5. What some people think George M. Cohan is. ~ EGOIST
D6. A young lawyer would like one of these. ~ CLIENT
D7. You might say this of some of those French post cards. ~ RACY
D8. What they are supposed to become after the ceremony. ~ ONE
D9. A Middle Western State (abr.). ~ SD
D10. A call—(You might learn this if you went to Switzerland.) ~ YODEL
D13. An artist who draws lovely girls every week in JUDGE. ~ PATTERSON
D16. A comic artist who draws for JUDGE. (You'll have to look through some of the other issues—he isn't in this one.) ~ MILTGROSS
D18. A city in Pennsylvania. ~ READING
D20. A conjunction meaning "on the condition"—spelled backwards. ~ FI
D21. You eat these at the circus—and you can't stop till they're all gone. ~ PEANUTS
D26. A prefix meaning new. ~ NEO
D28. A good way to get ahead ~ RUN
D32. One who resists. ~ REBEL
D33. Something which indicates. ~ INDEX
D34. Something the Germans used during the war which caused us some worry. ~ UBOAT
D36. First person—singular—as an Irishman says it in the comics. ~ OI
D38. Part of a blossom. ~ PETAL
D40. A familiar place of refuge— Biblical (plural). ~ ARKS
D41. On the level. ~ EVEN
D44. What the French girls say— everybody knows this much French. ~ OUI
D46. A period in history. ~ ERA
D48. What the boys in the army never want to be (abr.). ~ KP
D49. A Nation in the Western Hemisphere you may have heard of. ~ US
D50. Something it's hard to get over on a busy day (abr.). ~ ST


